  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quiet weather continues as temperatures warm up\nearly this week, next notable rain chance arrives Wednesday \nnight into Thursday.\n \nA weak upper-level shortwave passing through the area this\nmorning has resulted in some light showers/drizzle mainly west\nof the Blue Ridge. Dry air continues to erode the shower\nactivity preventing it from reaching any further east. Should\nsee these showers begin to wane by sunrise.\n\nHigh pressure pushes offshore into the Atlantic into Tuesday, \nwith the clockwise flow around the high bringing warmer \ntemperatures back into the area. Highs will climb into the 70s, \nwith some Piedmont locations approaching 80 by Wednesday ahead \nof the next system. The next system will move across the Upper \nMidwest and into the Great Lakes Region by Thursday, with the \naccompanying cold front bringing a chance of rain to our\nforecast area. However, models have continued to back off on \nprecipitation coverage, as the system and upper- level trough \nboth try to retreat farther north, resulting in less moisture \nand lift across our area. While the entire area is currently \nexpected to still receive some measurable rainfall, greatest\namounts look to be confined west of the Blue Ridge.\n\nMay see some lingering upslope showers along the western slopes\nthrough Saturday given persistent NW flow. Otherwise, quiet \nweather once again returns through next weekend, with \ntemperatures still slightly above normal but cooling off around \n5 degrees behind the front.",
